.TH DATE 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
date \- print or set the date and time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B date
.RB [ \-Ru ]
.RB [ \-D
.IR infmt ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR time ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-I\c
.RI [ spec ]]
.RB [ + \c
.IR outfmt ]
.br
.B date
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB [ \-D
.IR infmt ]
.RB [ + \c
.IR outfmt ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.I time
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B date
prints a time on standard output, or sets the system clock.
With no operands it prints the current time.
.B \-d
prints a given time instead, and
.B \-r
prints the modification time of a file.
A
.I time
operand or
.B \-s
sets the system clock to that time and then prints it.
.PP
The output is the formatted time and a newline.
An operand that begins with
.B +
is the output format
.IR outfmt ;
see
.BR "Output formats" .
It must come before a bare
.I time
operand, and it overrides
.B \-R
and
.BR \-I .
With none of these, the format is
.BR "%a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Z %Y" ,
as in
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
Mon Oct 19 20:29:49 Eastern Daylight Time 2026
.EE
.RE
.PP
With
.BR \-R ,
the format is that of RFC 2822, as in
.BR "Mon, 19 Oct 2026 20:29:49 \-0400" ,
and with
.BR \-I ,
that of ISO 8601.
.SS Time zone
Times are read and printed in the local time zone, which is the Windows
time zone setting unless
.B TZ
is set.
With
.BR \-u ,
the time zone is UTC.
.SS Time formats
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-s ,
and a
.I time
operand take a time in one of these forms.
It must match a form entirely.
Names are in English.
In the forms with separators, a field may be written with one or two
digits, except the year.
A date field that the time does not give is taken from the current date,
or from the modification time of the
.B \-r
file, and an hour, minute, or second field that it does not give is zero.
.TP
\fIhh\fB:\fImm\fR[\fB:\fIss\fR]
A time of day on the current date.
.TP
\fIMM\fB.\fIDD\fB\-\fIhh\fB:\fImm\fR[\fB:\fIss\fR]
A month, day, and time in the current year.
.TP
\fIYYYY\fB.\fIMM\fB.\fIDD\fB\-\fIhh\fB:\fImm\fR[\fB:\fIss\fR]
A full date and time.
.TP
\fIMon\fR \fIDD\fR \fIhh\fB:\fImm\fB:\fIss\fR \fIYYYY\fR
.I Mon
is a month name in full or abbreviated to 3 letters, in any case.
There is no weekday.
.TP
\fIYYYY\fB\-\fIMM\fB\-\fIDD\fR[ \fIhh\fR[\fB:\fImm\fR[\fB:\fIss\fR]]]
A date with a time of day, an hour, or neither.
.TP
\fIYYYY\fB\-\fIMM\fB\-\fIDD\fR \fIhh\fB:\fImm\fR[\fB:\fIss\fR] \fIzone\fR
A date and time in another time zone, converted to the local one.
The space before
.I zone
is optional.
.I zone
is
.BI + hhmm
or
.BI \- hhmm
for a UTC offset of up to 12 hours,
.BI + hh
or
.BI \- hh
for whole hours, or
.B Z
for UTC.
An offset is accurate to only 36 seconds, so minutes that are not a
multiple of 3 are inexact.
.TP
\fB@\fIseconds\fR
Seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC, as decimal digits with an
optional minus sign and nothing else.
.TP
[[[[[\fIYY\fR]\fIYY\fR]\fIMM\fR]\fIDD\fR]\fIhh\fR]\fImm\fR[\fB.\fIss\fR]
Digits only, in one of these forms:
.IR mm ,
.IR hhmm ,
.IR DDhhmm ,
.IR MMDDhhmm ,
.IR YYMMDDhhmm ,
or
.IR CCYYMMDDhhmm ,
followed by an optional
.BI . ss\fR.
.I YY
is the year within 50 years of the current year.
Each field is checked against its maximum, so month 13 is an error, but
month or day 00 is carried back into the previous year or month.
.PP
A seconds field of 60 is carried into the next minute, and so is 61
except in the digits-only form.
A day that the month lacks, such as February 30, is carried into the
next month.
An hour of 24 is an error.
.PP
.B \-D
replaces these forms with
.IR infmt ,
a format for the C function
.BR strptime .
Its conversions are
.BR %a ,
.BR %A ,
.BR %b ,
.BR %B ,
.BR %c ,
.BR %C ,
.BR %d ,
.BR %D ,
.BR %e ,
.BR %F ,
.BR %g ,
.BR %G ,
.BR %h ,
.BR %H ,
.BR %I ,
.BR %j ,
.BR %k ,
.BR %l ,
.BR %m ,
.BR %M ,
.BR %n ,
.BR %p ,
.BR %q ,
.BR %r ,
.BR %R ,
.BR %s ,
.BR %S ,
.BR %t ,
.BR %T ,
.BR %u ,
.BR %U ,
.BR %V ,
.BR %w ,
.BR %W ,
.BR %x ,
.BR %X ,
.BR %y ,
.BR %Y ,
.BR %z ,
.BR %Z ,
and
.BR %% .
The
.B E
modifier is ignored before
.BR c ,
.BR C ,
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
.BR y ,
or
.BR Y ,
and
.B O
before
.BR d ,
.BR e ,
.BR H ,
.BR I ,
.BR m ,
.BR M ,
.BR q ,
.BR S ,
.BR U ,
.BR V ,
.BR w ,
.BR W ,
or
.BR y ;
elsewhere either is an error.
A numeric conversion skips leading spaces and reads at most 2 digits,
but 1 for
.BR %q ,
.BR %u ,
and
.BR %w ,
3 for
.BR %j ,
and 4 for
.BR %Y .
.B %G
and
.B %s
read any number of digits and do not skip spaces.
White space in
.I infmt
matches any amount of white space in the input, including none.
Input left over once
.I infmt
is matched is ignored, so the input is not checked to the end.
.B %y
reads 69 to 99 as 1969 to 1999, and 00 to 68 as 2000 to 2068.
.B %s
sets the entire time from seconds since the epoch, as
.B @
does.
.BR %g ,
.BR %G ,
and
.B %V
have no effect,
.B %z
is parsed but its offset is ignored, and
.B %Z
matches nothing.
.BR %a ,
.BR %A ,
.BR %u ,
and
.B %w
have an effect only along with
.B %U
or
.BR %W ,
and the reverse.
.B %j
has an effect only along with a conversion that reads the year, month,
or day.
Fields that
.I infmt
does not set are as described above.
.SS Output formats
In
.IR outfmt ,
each conversion beginning with
.B %
is replaced, and everything else is copied.
Weekday and month names are in English.
These conversions are supported:
.TP
.B %%
A percent sign.
.TP
.BR %a ", " %A
The weekday name, abbreviated to 3 letters or in full.
.TP
.BR %b ", " %B
The month name, abbreviated to 3 letters or in full.
.TP
.B %c
The date and time as
.BR "11/14/23 17:13:20" :
month, day, and two-digit year, then the time.
.TP
.B %d
The day of the month, 01 to 31.
.TP
.B %e
The day of the month, 1 to 31, padded with a space.
.TP
.B %H
The hour, 00 to 23.
.TP
.B %I
The hour, 01 to 12.
.TP
.B %j
The day of the year, 001 to 366.
.TP
.B %m
The month, 01 to 12.
.TP
.B %M
The minute, 00 to 59.
.TP
.B %N
The nanoseconds, as 9 digits.
.TP
.BI % n N
The first
.I n
digits of
.BR %N ,
truncated, for
.I n
from 1 to 9.
A larger
.I n
pads
.B %N
on the left with zeros to
.I n
digits, and 0 means 9.
.IP
The nanoseconds are zero with
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-s ,
or a
.I time
operand, and with
.B \-r
they are those of the modification time.
.TP
.B %p
.B AM
or
.BR PM .
.TP
.B %s
Seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC.
.TP
.B %S
The second, 00 to 59.
.TP
.B %T
The same as
.BR %H:%M:%S .
.TP
.B %U
The week of the year, 00 to 53, with weeks starting on Sunday.
.TP
.B %w
The weekday, 0 to 6, with Sunday as 0.
.TP
.B %W
The week of the year, 00 to 53, with weeks starting on Monday.
.TP
.B %x
The date as
.BR 11/14/23 .
.TP
.B %X
The time as
.BR 17:13:20 .
.TP
.B %y
The year within the century, 00 to 99.
.TP
.B %Y
The year.
.TP
.B %z
The UTC offset as
.BI + hhmm
or
.BI \- hhmm\fR.
.TP
.B %Z
The time zone name.
With the Windows time zone it is the Windows name, such as
.BR "Eastern Daylight Time" .
With
.B TZ
it is the name in
.BR TZ ,
and with
.B \-u
it is
.BR UTC .
.PP
A
.B \-
after the
.B %
removes the padding of
.BR %d ,
.BR %H ,
.BR %I ,
.BR %j ,
.BR %m ,
.BR %M ,
.BR %S ,
.BR %U ,
.BR %w ,
.BR %W ,
.BR %y ,
and
.BR %Y ,
so
.B %\-d
is
.B 5
on the fifth.
A
.B #
does the same, and also gives
.B %c
and
.B %x
long forms such as
.BR "Tuesday, November 14, 2023" .
It is ignored with
.BR %a ,
.BR %A ,
.BR %b ,
.BR %B ,
.BR %p ,
.BR %X ,
and
.BR %Z ,
and
.B %#z
is the same as
.BR %Z .
.PP
No other conversion, flag, or field width is supported.
If
.I outfmt
has one, or the result is longer than 1023 bytes,
.B date
prints an empty line and exits with status 0.
An empty
.I outfmt
also prints an empty line.
.PP
The output of
.B \-I
depends on
.IR spec ,
and the offset is
.B +00:00
with
.BR \-u :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
date     2026\-10\-19
hours    2026\-10\-19T20\-04:00
minutes  2026\-10\-19T20:29\-04:00
seconds  2026\-10\-19T20:29:49\-04:00
ns       2026\-10\-19T20:29:49,033721000\-04:00
.EE
.RE
.SS Setting the clock
A
.I time
operand or
.B \-s
sets the system clock, reading the time as local time, or as UTC with
.BR \-u .
The new time is then printed as usual.
Setting the clock needs administrator rights.
Without them,
.B date
prints
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
date: can\(aqt set date: Operation not permitted
.EE
.RE
.PP
and exits with status 1.
That message is printed whenever Windows refuses to set the clock.
.PP
A bare
.I time
operand that is only digits, with an optional
.BI . ss
of exactly two digits, is read differently from the digits-only form
above: as
\fIMMDDhhmm\fR[[\fICC\fR]\fIYY\fR][\fB.\fIss\fR],
with the year last, which is the POSIX form.
It must have 8, 10, or 12 digits, and any other count is an error.
For example,
.B 1019123420
is 12:34 on 19 October 2020, but
.B \-s 1019123420
is an error, since it is read as
.IR YYMMDDhhmm ,
with month 19.
.B \-s 2010191234
sets the same time.
Such an operand is checked and reordered this way even with
.BR \-D ,
before
.I infmt
reads it.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-uR ,
and may follow the operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-d@0 ,
or be the next argument.
The exception is the optional argument of
.BR \-I ,
which must be attached, so in a group
.B \-I
comes last.
A long option may be abbreviated to any unambiguous prefix, and its
argument is attached with
.B =
or is the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-utc
Print or set the time in UTC.
For this command only, it sets
.B TZ
to
.BR UTC0 ,
overriding any
.BR TZ .
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fItime\fR, \fB\-\-set\fR \fItime\fR
Set the system clock to
.IR time ;
see
.BR "Setting the clock" .
Incompatible with
.BR \-d .
.TP
\fB\-d\fR \fItime\fR, \fB\-\-date\fR \fItime\fR
Print
.I time
instead of the current time.
Incompatible with
.BR \-s .
.TP
.BI "\-D " infmt
Read
.I time
with the
.B strptime
format
.I infmt
instead of the forms in
.BR "Time formats" .
It applies to the argument of
.B \-d
and
.BR \-s ,
and to a bare
.I time
operand, which sets the clock.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-reference\fR \fIfile\fR
Print the modification time of
.I file
instead of the current time.
If
.I file
cannot be examined, it is an error.
With a
.I time
to print or set, it only supplies the fields that
.I time
leaves out.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-rfc\-2822
Print the time in the form of RFC 2822:
.BR "%a, %d %b %Y %H:%M:%S %z" .
.B \-\-rfc\-822
is a synonym.
Incompatible with
.BR \-I .
.TP
\fB\-I\fR[\fIspec\fR]
Print the time in the form of ISO 8601, to the precision
.IR spec :
.B date
(the default),
.BR hours ,
.BR minutes ,
.BR seconds ,
or
.BR ns ,
which adds nanoseconds after a comma.
All but
.B date
end with the UTC offset, as in
.BR \-04:00 .
.I spec
may be abbreviated to any unambiguous prefix, such as
.B h
or
.BR n .
Any other value is an error.
Incompatible with
.BR \-R .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must precede the operands.
.TP
.B TZ
The local time zone.
The format is that of the Microsoft C runtime: a three-letter zone name,
the hours to add to local time to give UTC, optionally followed by
minutes and seconds after colons, and an optional three-letter daylight
saving zone name, as in
.BR EST5EDT ,
.BR JST\-9 ,
or
.BR IST\-5:30 .
The offset is read from the fourth character, so a longer standard name
is misread: in
.B CEST\-2
the zone is
.B CES
at UTC and the daylight saving name is
.BR T\-2 .
A longer daylight saving name is cut to three letters.
Daylight saving time in a
.B TZ
that names it follows the United States rules, whatever the zone.
A value the C runtime rejects, such as
.B Europe/Paris
or one beginning with a colon, is ignored, as is an empty value.
Then the Windows time zone setting applies.
.B \-u
sets it to
.B UTC0
for the command.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or operand, with a usage message on standard error.
That includes
.B \-d
with
.BR \-s ,
.B \-R
with
.BR \-I ,
an unknown
.B \-I
argument, and an extra operand.
Also a
.I time
that matches no form, or is outside the supported range, reported as
an invalid date; a
.B \-r
file that cannot be examined; and a clock that cannot be set.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the current time in UTC in ISO 8601 form:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ date \-u +%Y\-%m\-%dT%H:%M:%SZ
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the time of a number of seconds since the epoch:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ date \-u \-d @1700000000
Tue Nov 14 22:13:20 UTC 2023
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the seconds since the epoch of a time in UTC, then the current
time in milliseconds since the epoch:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ date \-u \-d \(aq2026\-10\-19 12:00\(aq +%s
1792411200
$ date +%s%3N
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read a date in another form, and print one in another form:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ date \-D %d/%m/%Y \-d 19/10/2026 +%Y\-%m\-%d
2026\-10\-19
$ date \-u \-d @1700000000 \(aq+%A, %B %\-d, %Y\(aq
Tuesday, November 14, 2023
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the modification time of a file with its UTC offset:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ date \-r main.c \-Iseconds
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print a time in the time zone of Japan:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ TZ=JST\-9 date \-d @1700000000
Wed Nov 15 07:13:20 JST 2023
.EE
.RE
.PP
Set the clock, from an elevated console, in two ways:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ date \-s \(aq2026\-10\-19 12:00:00\(aq
$ date 101912002026
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR date :
.IP \(bu 2
The only long options are
.BR \-\-date ,
.BR \-\-reference ,
.BR \-\-rfc\-2822 ,
.BR \-\-rfc\-822 ,
.BR \-\-set ,
and
.BR \-\-utc .
There is no
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-\-iso\-8601 ,
.BR \-\-rfc\-3339 ,
or
.BR \-\-universal .
.IP \(bu 2
A time is not free-form text.
Words such as
.B now
and
.BR yesterday ,
relative times such as
.BR "1 day ago" ,
weekday names, a
.B T
between date and time, fractional seconds, time zone names, and
.B AM
or
.B PM
are errors.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-D
is a busybox extension.
.IP \(bu 2
The output conversions are only those above.
Others that GNU
.B date
has print an empty line, such as
.BR %C ,
.BR %D ,
.BR %F ,
.BR %g ,
.BR %G ,
.BR %h ,
.BR %k ,
.BR %l ,
.BR %n ,
.BR %P ,
.BR %r ,
.BR %R ,
.BR %t ,
.BR %u ,
and
.BR %V ,
as do the
.BR _ ,
.BR 0 ,
and
.B ^
flags and field widths.
.PP
Only times from 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC to 3001-01-01 07:59:59 UTC,
which are 0 through 32535244799 seconds since the epoch, can be
converted.
A
.I time
outside that range, written in a form other than
.BR @ ,
is an invalid date.
In the form with a
.IR zone ,
the latest time is 3000-12-31 23:59:59 UTC.
An
.BI @ seconds
outside the range, including every negative value, or a
.B %s
value outside it with
.BR \-D ,
is not an error but is taken to be the epoch, 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC,
which is then printed, or with
.B \-s
set.
.PP
The names of the days and months are English whatever the system
language, and
.BR %c ,
.BR %x ,
and
.B %X
have fixed forms.
.PP
The nanoseconds of the current time are those of the Windows system
clock, in multiples of 100.
.PP
Paths for
.B \-r
may use forward or back slashes.
.PP
Lines end with LF, not CRLF.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cal (1),
.BR stat (1),
.BR touch (1)
